==English==

===Etymology===
Of uncertain origin. These possibilities have been suggested:
* {{etyl|ga|en}} {{term|sionnachuighim|lang=ga||I play the fox}}.
* {{etyl|es|en}} {{term|chanada||lang=es}}, a shortening of {{term|charranada||lang=es|trick, deceit}}.
* {{etyl|pfl|en}} {{term|schinägeln||lang=pfl|to work hard}}, from the peddler's argot term {{m|pfl||Schenigelei|work}}.
* {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|manigance|ces manigances|lang=fr|these fraudulent schemes}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ʃəˈnænɪgən/|lang=en}},
* {{audio|shenanigan_us.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|countable|lang=en}} A deceitful confidence trick, or mischief causing discomfort or annoyance.
#: ''I spotted his next '''shenanigan''' — saw it coming — and so avoided being fooled.''
# {{label|en|rare}} {{singular of|shenanigans|lang=en}}

